(window.CE_PACK=window.CE_PACK||{})["Object-Oriented Programming"]=Object.assign((window.CE_PACK||{})["Object-Oriented Programming"]||{},{docs:{"assign":"# Object-Oriented Programming (C++): Assignments with Worked Solutions\n\nType and run every program yourself with `g++ -std=c++17 -Wall -Wextra -g`. Assume `#include` lines for `<iostream>`, `<string>`, `<vector>`, `<memory>`, `<algorithm>`, `<numeric>`, `<functional>`, `<stdexcept>`, `<map>` and `using namespace std;` for brevity in the exercises. The \"predict the output\" answers below were derived by tracing the C++17 rules by hand (check them by running the code). Marks are awarded for correctness, clear design, const-correctness and resource safety.\n\n# Assignment 1: Classes, Object Lifetime and Value Semantics (60 marks)\n\n## Questions\n\n**Q1. Encapsulation (10 marks)** Design a class BankAccount with a private owner and balance, a constructor, deposit (throws invalid_argument if the amount is not positive), withdraw (returns false and does nothing if the amount is not positive or exceeds the balance), and const accessors. State the class invariant. Predict the output of:\n```cpp\nBankAccount a(\"Ama\", 100.0);\na.deposit(50);\nbool ok1 = a.withdraw(200);\nbool ok2 = a.withdraw(30);\ncout << a.balance() << \" \" << ok1 << \" \" << ok2 << \"\\n\";\ntry { a.deposit(-5); } catch (const invalid_argument& e) { cout << e.what() << \"\\n\"; }\n```\n\n**Q2. Construction and destruction order (10 marks)** (a) Predict the output [4]:\n```cpp\nstruct A { A() { cout << \"A+ \"; } ~A() { cout << \"A- \"; } };\nstruct B : A { B() { cout << \"B+ \"; } ~B() { cout << \"B- \"; } };\nstruct C { A a; B b; C() { cout << \"C+ \"; } ~C() { cout << \"C- \"; } };\nint main() { C c; cout << \"| \"; }\n```\n(b) Predict the output of the following (C++17) and explain each copy [6]:\n```cpp\nstruct Tracer {\n    string n;\n    Tracer(string s) : n(s) { cout << n << \"+ \"; }\n    Tracer(const Tracer& o) : n(o.n + \"'\") { cout << n << \"c \"; }\n    ~Tracer() { cout << n << \"- \"; }\n};\nTracer make() { return Tracer(\"t\"); }\nvoid byValue(Tracer x) {}\nvoid byRef(const Tracer& x) {}\nint main() {\n    Tracer a(\"a\");\n    byValue(a);\n    byRef(a);\n    Tracer b = make();\n    cout << \"end \";\n}\n```\n\n**Q3. Rule of three (12 marks)** The class below owns a raw array.\n```cpp\nclass IntArray {\npublic:\n    explicit IntArray(size_t n) : n_(n), data_(new int[n]()) {}\n    ~IntArray() { delete[] data_; }\n    int& operator[](size_t i) { return data_[i]; }\n    size_t size() const { return n_; }\nprivate:\n    size_t n_;\n    int* data_;\n};\n```\n(a) Explain what goes wrong with `IntArray y = x;` as written [3]. (b) Add the copy constructor and copy assignment operator [5]. (c) Add the move constructor and explain the benefit [2]. (d) Predict the output with your fixed class [2]:\n```cpp\nIntArray x(3); x[0] = 1; x[1] = 2; x[2] = 3;\nIntArray y = x;\ny[0] = 99;\ncout << x[0] << \" \" << y[0] << \" \" << x.size();\nIntArray z(1); z = x; z[2] = 7;\ncout << \" \" << x[2] << \" \" << z[2];\n```\n\n**Q4. Operator overloading (12 marks)** Write a class Vec2 with a constructor, `+`, `+=`, `dot`, `==` and a stream insertion operator. Then predict the output of:\n```cpp\nVec2 a(1, 2), b(3, 4);\nVec2 c = a + b;\nc += Vec2(1, 1);\ncout << c << \" \" << a.dot(b) << \" \" << (a == b) << \"\\n\";\n```\nExplain why `operator<<` is not a member function and why `+=` returns a reference.\n\n**Q5. Static members (8 marks)** Predict the output and explain:\n```cpp\nclass Widget {\npublic:\n    Widget() { ++count_; id_ = count_; }\n    ~Widget() { --count_; }\n    int id() const { return id_; }\n    static int count() { return count_; }\nprivate:\n    static int count_;\n    int id_;\n};\nint Widget::count_ = 0;\nint main() {\n    Widget a;\n    { Widget b, c; cout << Widget::count() << \" \" << c.id() << \" \"; }\n    Widget d;\n    cout << Widget::count() << \" \" << d.id();\n}\n```\n\n**Q6. References and pointers (8 marks)** Predict the output and explain each parameter kind:\n```cpp\nvoid f(int a, int& b, int* c) { a++; b++; (*c)++; c = nullptr; }\nint main() { int x = 1, y = 1, z = 1; f(x, y, &z); cout << x << y << z; }\n```\nThen rewrite f so that the pointer parameter is replaced by a reference and a const reference is used for a `const string&` argument example.\n\n## Solutions and Marking Guide\n\n**Q1.**\n```cpp\nclass BankAccount {\npublic:\n    BankAccount(string owner, double balance = 0.0)\n        : owner_(std::move(owner)), balance_(balance) {\n        if (balance_ < 0) throw invalid_argument(\"negative opening balance\");\n    }\n    void deposit(double amount) {\n        if (amount <= 0) throw invalid_argument(\"amount must be positive\");\n        balance_ += amount;\n    }\n    bool withdraw(double amount) {\n        if (amount <= 0 || amount > balance_) return false;\n        balance_ -= amount;\n        return true;\n    }\n    double balance() const { return balance_; }\n    const string& owner() const { return owner_; }\nprivate:\n    string owner_;\n    double balance_;\n};\n```\n**Invariant:** balance_ ≥ 0 at all times, enforced by the constructor and by every mutator. Output: the balance becomes 150 after the deposit; withdraw(200) fails (false), withdraw(30) succeeds (true) leaving 120. So the first line is **120 0 1** and the second is **amount must be positive**. (3 marks design with private data, 3 invariant checks, 2 const accessors, 2 output.)\n\n**Q2.**\n(a) The members of C are built in declaration order: a prints \"A+ \"; then b is built: its base A first (\"A+ \"), then B's body (\"B+ \"); then C's body (\"C+ \"). After \"| \" the destruction runs in reverse: C's body (\"C- \"), then members in reverse order: b (\"B- \" then its base \"A- \"), then a (\"A- \"). Output: **A+ A+ B+ C+ | C- B- A- A- **.\n(b) `a` is constructed (\"a+ \"). byValue(a) copy-constructs the parameter: its name becomes \"a'\" (\"a'c \") and it is destroyed when the call ends (\"a'- \"). byRef(a) makes no copy. `Tracer b = make();` uses guaranteed copy elision (C++17): the temporary is constructed directly in b, so only \"t+ \" is printed, with no copy or move. Then \"end \". At the end of main the objects are destroyed in reverse order: b (\"t- \") then a (\"a- \"). Output: **a+ a'c a'- t+ end t- a- **.\n\n**Q3.**\n(a) The compiler-generated copy constructor copies the pointer data_ (a shallow copy), so x and y share the same array. Modifying y modifies x, and when both go out of scope delete[] runs twice on the same memory: **double free, undefined behaviour**.\n(b)\n```cpp\nIntArray(const IntArray& o) : n_(o.n_), data_(new int[o.n_]) {\n    std::copy(o.data_, o.data_ + n_, data_);\n}\nIntArray& operator=(const IntArray& o) {\n    if (this != &o) {\n        int* p = new int[o.n_];                 // allocate first: strong exception safety\n        std::copy(o.data_, o.data_ + o.n_, p);\n        delete[] data_;\n        data_ = p;\n        n_ = o.n_;\n    }\n    return *this;\n}\n```\n(c)\n```cpp\nIntArray(IntArray&& o) noexcept : n_(o.n_), data_(o.data_) { o.n_ = 0; o.data_ = nullptr; }\n```\nThe move constructor steals the pointer from a temporary (an rvalue), avoiding an allocation and a copy: returning an IntArray from a function or growing a vector<IntArray> becomes O(1) per element instead of O(n). The moved-from object is left empty but valid (and its destructor safely deletes nullptr).\n(d) With deep copies: x[0] stays 1 while y[0] = 99, size 3: the first part prints \"1 99 3\". Then z = x copies the contents, z[2] = 7 does not affect x, so x[2] = 3 and z[2] = 7. Output: **1 99 3 3 7**.\n\n**Q4.**\n```cpp\nclass Vec2 {\npublic:\n    Vec2(double x = 0, double y = 0) : x_(x), y_(y) {}\n    Vec2 operator+(const Vec2& o) const { return Vec2(x_ + o.x_, y_ + o.y_); }\n    Vec2& operator+=(const Vec2& o) { x_ += o.x_; y_ += o.y_; return *this; }\n    double dot(const Vec2& o) const { return x_ * o.x_ + y_ * o.y_; }\n    bool operator==(const Vec2& o) const { return x_ == o.x_ && y_ == o.y_; }\n    friend ostream& operator<<(ostream& os, const Vec2& v) {\n        return os << \"(\" << v.x_ << \", \" << v.y_ << \")\";\n    }\nprivate:\n    double x_, y_;\n};\n```\nc = (1 + 3, 2 + 4) = (4, 6); after `+= (1, 1)` it is (5, 7); a · b = 3 + 8 = 11; a == b is false (0). Output: **(5, 7) 11 0**. `operator<<` must take the stream as its left operand, which is not a Vec2, so it cannot be a member of Vec2 (it is a free function, usually a friend to reach the private data). `+=` returns `*this` by reference so that calls can be chained (`a += b += c`) and it behaves like the built-in operator without a copy.\n\n**Q5.** a: count 1, id 1. b: count 2, id 2. c: count 3, id 3: the first print is \"3 3 \". At the end of the block c and b are destroyed (count back to 1). d: count 2, id 2. Output: **3 3 2 2**. The static member count_ is shared by all objects, so ids are assigned from the running count; note that ids are reused, which is a design flaw of using a live count for identity (a separate static next-id counter would give unique ids).\n\n**Q6.** a is passed by value: the increment changes a copy, so x stays 1. b is a reference: it aliases y, so y becomes 2. c is a pointer to z: `(*c)++` changes z to 2; the assignment `c = nullptr` changes only the local copy of the pointer. Output: **122**. Rewrite: `void f(int a, int& b, int& c) { a++; b++; c++; }` called as `f(x, y, z)`, and `void print(const string& s)` to avoid copying a large string while promising not to modify it.\n\n---\n\n# Assignment 2: Inheritance and Polymorphism (70 marks)\n\n## Questions\n\n**Q1. Virtual dispatch (8 marks)** Predict the output and explain each line:\n```cpp\nstruct Base {\n    void f() { cout << \"Base::f \"; }\n    virtual void g() { cout << \"Base::g \"; }\n    virtual ~Base() = default;\n};\nstruct Der : Base {\n    void f() { cout << \"Der::f \"; }\n    void g() override { cout << \"Der::g \"; }\n};\nint main() {\n    Der d; Base* p = &d;\n    p->f(); p->g(); d.f();\n    Base b = d; b.g();\n}\n```\n\n**Q2. A shape hierarchy (14 marks)** Write an abstract class Shape with pure virtual `double area() const` and `string name() const`, a virtual destructor, and concrete classes Rect(w, h), Circle(r) (use π = 3.14) and Triangle(base, height). Store Rect(3, 4), Circle(2) and Triangle(6, 5) in a `vector<unique_ptr<Shape>>`, print the total area and the name of the largest shape. Explain why unique_ptr and a base-class pointer are used.\n\n**Q3. Virtual destructors (8 marks)**\n```cpp\nstruct Base { ~Base() { cout << \"Base~ \"; } };\nstruct Der : Base { ~Der() { cout << \"Der~ \"; } };\nint main() { Base* p = new Der; delete p; }\n```\n(a) Explain the problem and what is printed in practice [4]. (b) Show the fix and the output [4].\n\n**Q4. Constructors in a hierarchy (8 marks)** Predict the output:\n```cpp\nstruct P {\n    P()      { cout << \"P \"; }\n    P(int)   { cout << \"Pi \"; }\n    virtual void who() { cout << \"P \"; }\n    virtual ~P() = default;\n};\nstruct Q : P {\n    Q()      { cout << \"Q \"; }\n    Q(int x) : P(x) { cout << \"Qi \"; }\n    void who() override { cout << \"Q \"; }\n};\nstruct R {\n    P p; Q q;\n    R() : q(5) { cout << \"R \"; }\n};\nint main() { R r; r.p.who(); r.q.who(); }\n```\n\n**Q5. Composition or inheritance (8 marks)** For each pair decide \"is-a\" or \"has-a\" and justify: (a) Car and Engine; (b) Student and Person; (c) Stack and vector; (d) Square and Rectangle. Give a design for a Stack built on a vector and explain why public inheritance from vector would be a mistake.\n\n**Q6. Liskov substitution and SOLID (10 marks)** (a) A class Square derives from Rectangle (setWidth/setHeight) and overrides the setters to keep the sides equal. Show a function that works for Rectangle and fails for Square, and name the violated principle [4]. (b) Propose a better design [3]. (c) The function `double totalPay(vector<Employee*>& v)` contains `if (e->type == \"manager\") ... else if (e->type == \"engineer\") ...`. Which principle does it violate and how do you fix it? [3]\n\n**Q7. UML (6 marks)** Draw (describe in text) a class diagram for: a Library has many Books (books cannot exist without the library); Members borrow Books (a member may borrow many, a book is borrowed by at most one member at a time); Staff is a kind of Member who can also add Books. Label the relationships and multiplicities.\n\n**Q8. Casts and type identification (8 marks)** Using Base and Der from Q1, predict the output and explain:\n```cpp\nBase* p = new Der;\nif (Der* d = dynamic_cast<Der*>(p)) cout << \"is Der \";\nBase* q = new Base;\ncout << (dynamic_cast<Der*>(q) == nullptr);\ndelete p; delete q;\n```\n\n## Solutions and Marking Guide\n\n**Q1.** p->f(): f is **not virtual**, so the call uses the static type (Base*): \"Base::f \". p->g(): virtual, so the dynamic type (Der) decides: \"Der::g \". d.f(): d's static type is Der, so \"Der::f \" (Der::f hides Base::f). `Base b = d;` **slices** d into a Base object (only the Base subobject is copied); b.g() therefore calls Base::g: \"Base::g \". Output: **Base::f Der::g Der::f Base::g ** (2 marks per line).\n\n**Q2.**\n```cpp\nclass Shape {\npublic:\n    virtual double area() const = 0;\n    virtual string name() const = 0;\n    virtual ~Shape() = default;\n};\nclass Rect : public Shape {\npublic:\n    Rect(double w, double h) : w_(w), h_(h) {}\n    double area() const override { return w_ * h_; }\n    string name() const override { return \"rect\"; }\nprivate:\n    double w_, h_;\n};\nclass Circle : public Shape {\npublic:\n    explicit Circle(double r) : r_(r) {}\n    double area() const override { return 3.14 * r_ * r_; }\n    string name() const override { return \"circle\"; }\nprivate:\n    double r_;\n};\nclass Triangle : public Shape {\npublic:\n    Triangle(double b, double h) : b_(b), h_(h) {}\n    double area() const override { return 0.5 * b_ * h_; }\n    string name() const override { return \"triangle\"; }\nprivate:\n    double b_, h_;\n};\n\nint main() {\n    vector<unique_ptr<Shape>> shapes;\n    shapes.push_back(make_unique<Rect>(3, 4));\n    shapes.push_back(make_unique<Circle>(2));\n    shapes.push_back(make_unique<Triangle>(6, 5));\n    double total = 0;\n    const Shape* largest = shapes[0].get();\n    for (const auto& s : shapes) {\n        total += s->area();\n        if (s->area() > largest->area()) largest = s.get();\n    }\n    cout << \"total=\" << total << \" largest=\" << largest->name() << \"\\n\";\n}\n```\nStep 1: Areas: 12, 12.56, 15.\nStep 2: Output: **total=39.56 largest=triangle**.\nStep 3: A container of `Shape*` is needed because a `vector<Shape>` cannot hold an abstract class (and would slice concrete ones); `unique_ptr` makes the vector the sole owner, so every object is deleted exactly once (through the virtual destructor) and no manual delete is needed (4 marks hierarchy, 3 container and loop, 2 output, 3 explanation, 2 const/override use.)\n\n**Q3.**\n(a) Deleting a Der object through a Base* when Base's destructor is **not virtual** is **undefined behaviour**: the compiler calls only ~Base() (statically chosen), so the Der destructor never runs and Der's resources leak; in practice the output is **Base~ ** only (the standard allows anything).\n(b) Declare the base destructor virtual:\n```cpp\nstruct Base { virtual ~Base() { cout << \"Base~ \"; } };\n```\nThe delete now dispatches dynamically: **Der~ Base~ ** (derived destructor first, then the base). Rule: any class intended as a polymorphic base needs a virtual (or protected non-virtual) destructor.\n\n**Q4.** R's members are constructed in declaration order: p uses the default constructor, printing \"P \"; q is built with the argument 5: its base P(5) runs first (\"Pi \"), then Q(int)'s body (\"Qi \"); then R's body (\"R \"). Then r.p.who() prints \"P \" and r.q.who() prints \"Q \". Output: **P Pi Qi R P Q **.\n\n**Q5.** (a) **has-a** (an Engine is part of a Car; composition). (b) **is-a** (a Student is a Person; public inheritance, substitutable). (c) **has-a**: a Stack *uses* a vector for storage but must not expose push_front, insert, etc. (d) Mathematically is-a, but behaviourally problematic (see Q6): prefer a common Shape interface. A Stack design: `template <class T> class Stack { vector<T> items_; public: void push(const T&); T pop(); bool empty() const; };`. Public inheritance from vector would expose the whole vector interface (violating the stack's invariant \"access only at the top\"), and vector has no virtual destructor, so deleting through a vector* would be undefined behaviour.\n\n**Q6.**\n(a) With `void resize(Rectangle& r) { r.setWidth(5); r.setHeight(4); assert(r.area() == 20); }` the assertion holds for a Rectangle, but for a Square the second setter also changes the width, so the area is 16 and the assertion fails. A Square is not substitutable for a Rectangle in this contract: it violates the **Liskov Substitution Principle** (derived classes must honour the base class's behavioural contract).\n(b) Make Rectangle and Square independent classes implementing a common abstract Shape interface (with area()), or make shapes immutable value objects (`Rectangle(w, h)`, `Square(side)`) so no setters can break the contract.\n(c) It violates the **Open/Closed Principle**: adding an employee type needs editing the function. Fix: make `virtual double pay() const = 0;` in Employee, override it in Manager and Engineer, and let totalPay simply sum e->pay() (polymorphism replaces the type switch).\n\n**Q7.** Classes: Library, Book, Member, Staff. **Library ◆──── 0..* Book** (composition: a filled diamond at Library, since books do not exist without it). **Member 0..1 ──── 0..* Book** labelled \"borrows\" (an association: a member borrows many books; a book is borrowed by at most one member at a time). **Staff ──▷ Member** (inheritance: hollow triangle at Member). Staff has an operation `addBook(Book)` that Member lacks; Library has `addBook`, `findBook`. Attributes: Book(title, isbn), Member(name, id).\n\n**Q8.** The dynamic type of p is Der, so dynamic_cast<Der*>(p) succeeds: prints \"is Der \". q points to a plain Base, so the cast fails and returns nullptr: the comparison is true, printing 1. Output: **is Der 1**. (dynamic_cast requires a polymorphic base class and uses run-time type information; down-casting is often a sign that a virtual function would be a better design.)\n\n---\n\n# Assignment 3: Templates, the STL, Design Patterns and Exceptions (80 marks)\n\n## Questions\n\n**Q1. A class template (12 marks)** Write `template <typename T> class Stack` with push, pop (throws `out_of_range(\"empty stack\")` when empty), empty and size. Predict the output:\n```cpp\nStack<int> s;\ns.push(1); s.push(2); s.push(3);\nint a = s.pop(); int b = s.pop();\ncout << a << b << \" \" << s.size();\ntry { s.pop(); s.pop(); } catch (const out_of_range& e) { cout << \" \" << e.what(); }\n```\n\n**Q2. Function templates (8 marks)** Given `template <typename T> T maxOf(T a, T b) { return (a > b) ? a : b; }`: (a) What do `maxOf(3, 7)`, `maxOf(2.5, 1.5)` and `maxOf<double>(3, 2.5)` return? [3] (b) Why does `maxOf(3, 2.5)` not compile? [2] (c) What is wrong with `maxOf(\"apple\", \"pear\")`, and how do you fix it? [3]\n\n**Q3. STL algorithms (12 marks)** Trace the contents and results:\n```cpp\nvector<int> v = {5, 2, 8, 1, 9, 3};\nsort(v.begin(), v.end());\nint sum = accumulate(v.begin(), v.end(), 0);\nv.erase(remove_if(v.begin(), v.end(), [](int x) { return x % 2 == 0; }), v.end());\nauto it = find(v.begin(), v.end(), 5);\nint idx = it - v.begin();\nint c = count_if(v.begin(), v.end(), [](int x) { return x > 2; });\ntransform(v.begin(), v.end(), v.begin(), [](int x) { return x * x; });\n```\nGive sum, the vector after the erase, idx, c and the vector after transform. Explain the erase-remove idiom.\n\n**Q4. Associative containers (8 marks)** Count the words of \"a b a c b a\" with `map<string,int>`; print the entries in iteration order. State the complexity of insertion in map and in unordered_map, and when you would choose each.\n\n**Q5. Lambdas (6 marks)** Predict and explain:\n```cpp\nint k = 10;\nauto add = [k](int x) { return x + k; };\nk = 20;\nauto addr = [&k](int x) { return x + k; };\nk = 30;\ncout << add(1) << \" \" << addr(1);\n```\n\n**Q6. Design patterns (16 marks)** (a) Trace the Observer example [5]:\n```cpp\nclass Subject {\npublic:\n    void subscribe(function<void(int)> f) { obs_.push_back(std::move(f)); }\n    void set(int v) { for (auto& o : obs_) o(v); }\nprivate:\n    vector<function<void(int)>> obs_;\n};\nint main() {\n    Subject s; int total = 0;\n    s.subscribe([&](int v) { total += v; });\n    s.subscribe([](int v) { cout << \"got \" << v << \" \"; });\n    s.set(3); s.set(4);\n    cout << total;\n}\n```\n(b) Use the Strategy pattern: `void sortBy(vector<int>& v, function<bool(int,int)> cmp)` and show the result for {3, 1, 2} with a descending comparator [3]. (c) Write a thread-safe Singleton and list two drawbacks [4]. (d) Write a Factory function `unique_ptr<Shape> make(const string& kind, double a, double b)` for the Shape classes of Assignment 2 and explain its benefit [4].\n\n**Q7. Exceptions and RAII (10 marks)** Predict the output and explain the order:\n```cpp\nstruct G {\n    string n;\n    G(string s) : n(s) { cout << n << \"+ \"; }\n    ~G() { cout << n << \"- \"; }\n};\nvoid f() { G a(\"a\"); throw runtime_error(\"boom\"); G b(\"b\"); }\nint main() {\n    try { G m(\"m\"); f(); }\n    catch (const exception& e) { cout << e.what() << \" \"; }\n    cout << \"done\";\n}\n```\n\n**Q8. Smart pointers (8 marks)** (a) Predict the output [4]:\n```cpp\nauto a = make_shared<int>(5);\nauto b = a;\ncout << a.use_count();\n{ auto c = a; cout << a.use_count(); }\ncout << a.use_count();\n```\n(b) Predict the output [2]: `unique_ptr<int> p = make_unique<int>(7); unique_ptr<int> q = std::move(p); cout << (p == nullptr) << *q;` (c) How does weak_ptr break a reference cycle? [2]\n\n## Solutions and Marking Guide\n\n**Q1.**\n```cpp\ntemplate <typename T>\nclass Stack {\npublic:\n    void push(const T& v) { items_.push_back(v); }\n    T pop() {\n        if (items_.empty()) throw out_of_range(\"empty stack\");\n        T v = std::move(items_.back());\n        items_.pop_back();\n        return v;\n    }\n    bool empty() const { return items_.empty(); }\n    size_t size() const { return items_.size(); }\nprivate:\n    vector<T> items_;\n};\n```\nTrace: pushes 1, 2, 3; the first pop returns 3, the second 2, so \"32\" is printed, then a space and the size 1: \"32 1\". In the try block the first pop returns 1 (the stack is empty), the second throws `out_of_range`, and the catch prints \" empty stack\". Output: **32 1 empty stack**.\n\n**Q2.**\n(a) **7**, **2.5**, and **3** (the explicit `<double>` converts the arguments 3 and 2.5 to double; the result is 3.0, printed as 3).\n(b) Template argument deduction finds T = int from the first argument and T = double from the second: the two deductions conflict, so the call is ill-formed. Fix: `maxOf<double>(3, 2.5)` or cast one argument.\n(c) The arguments have type `const char*`, so T = const char* and `a > b` **compares pointer addresses**, not the strings: the result is unpredictable (not \"pear\"). Fix: convert to string (`maxOf(string(\"apple\"), string(\"pear\"))`), use `string_view`, or provide an overload or a template specialisation for `const char*` using `strcmp`.\n\n**Q3.** Step 1: After sort: 1 2 3 5 8 9.\nStep 2: **sum = 28** (5 + 2 + 8 + 1 + 9 + 3).\nStep 3: After the erase of even numbers: **1 3 5 9**. find returns the iterator to 5, so **idx = 2**. count_if (x > 2): 3, 5, 9, so **c = 3**.\nStep 4: After transform: **1 9 25 81**.\nStep 5: The **erase-remove idiom:** `remove_if` shifts the elements to keep towards the front and returns an iterator to the new logical end, but it cannot change the container's size; `erase(newEnd, end)` then physically removes the leftover tail (In C++20 `std::erase_if(v, pred)` does both.)\n\n**Q4.** Counts: a = 3, b = 2, c = 1. `map` iterates in sorted key order: **a 3, b 2, c 1**. map (balanced tree) insertion is **O(log n)** and keeps sorted order; unordered_map (hash table) is **O(1) average** (O(n) worst case) with no ordering. Choose map when sorted traversal, range queries or deterministic order matter; choose unordered_map for fastest lookup by key.\n\n**Q5.** `[k]` captures k **by value at the moment the lambda is created** (10), so add(1) = 11 even though k later changes. `[&k]` captures by reference, so it sees the current value of k at call time (30): addr(1) = 31. Output: **11 31**. (Capturing a reference to a local that has gone out of scope leaves a dangling reference.)\n\n**Q6.**\n(a) set(3) calls the first observer (total = 3) and then the second (prints \"got 3 \"); set(4) adds 4 (total = 7) and prints \"got 4 \". Finally the total is printed. Output: **got 3 got 4 7**.\n(b)\n```cpp\nvoid sortBy(vector<int>& v, function<bool(int, int)> cmp) { sort(v.begin(), v.end(), cmp); }\nvector<int> v = {3, 1, 2};\nsortBy(v, [](int a, int b) { return a > b; });      // descending: 3 2 1\n```\nResult: **3 2 1**. The algorithm (sorting) is fixed, the comparison strategy is injected.\n(c)\n```cpp\nclass Config {\npublic:\n    static Config& instance() { static Config c; return c; }   // initialised once, thread-safe since C++11\n    Config(const Config&) = delete;\n    Config& operator=(const Config&) = delete;\nprivate:\n    Config() = default;\n};\n```\nDrawbacks: it is **global state** (hidden dependencies, hard to reason about), and it **hinders testing** (cannot be replaced by a mock; state leaks between tests); it also couples every user to a concrete class and complicates multi-threading and shutdown order.\n(d)\n```cpp\nunique_ptr<Shape> make(const string& kind, double a, double b) {\n    if (kind == \"rect\")     return make_unique<Rect>(a, b);\n    if (kind == \"circle\")   return make_unique<Circle>(a);\n    if (kind == \"triangle\") return make_unique<Triangle>(a, b);\n    throw invalid_argument(\"unknown shape: \" + kind);\n}\n```\nBenefit: callers depend only on the Shape interface; the choice of concrete class is made in one place (for example from a file or user input), and ownership is explicit. Adding a new shape changes only the factory.\n\n**Q7.** m is constructed (\"m+ \"), f() constructs a (\"a+ \"), then throws; the line creating b is never reached, so b is never constructed. During **stack unwinding** the destructors of the fully constructed local objects run in reverse order of construction as the exception propagates: first a (\"a- \") as f exits, then m (\"m- \") as the try block is left. Then the handler prints \"boom \" and finally \"done\". Output: **m+ a+ a- m- boom done**. This guaranteed cleanup is why resources must be owned by objects (RAII).\n\n**Q8.**\n(a) After `auto b = a;` the use_count is 2, so the first print is 2; inside the block `c` is a third owner, so 3 is printed; after the block c is destroyed, so 2. Output: **2 3 2**. (The three print statements show 2, 3, 2.)\n(b) After the move, p is null (prints 1) and q owns the int (prints 7). Output: **17**.\n(c) In a cycle (A holds shared_ptr to B and B holds shared_ptr to A) the reference counts never reach zero and the objects leak. Making one direction a `weak_ptr` (a non-owning reference that does not increase the count; use `lock()` to obtain a shared_ptr if the object still exists) breaks the cycle.\n","exam":"# Object-Oriented Programming (C++): Final Examination\n\nDuration: 3 hours. No computer, no notes. Total: 100 marks. Assume the usual standard `#include` lines and `using namespace std;` in code fragments. Output answers must show exact spacing as printed.\n- Section A: 20 multiple-choice questions, 1 mark each (20 marks). Answer all.\n- Section B: 8 short questions, 5 marks each (40 marks). Answer all.\n- Section C: 5 long questions, 10 marks each. Answer any four (40 marks).\n\n## Section A: Multiple Choice (20 marks)\n\n**1.** In a C++ `class`, members are by default\nA) public  B) private  C) protected  D) static\n\n**2.** In a C++ `struct`, members are by default\nA) public  B) private  C) protected  D) const\n\n**3.** Data members are initialised in the order\nA) of the member initialiser list  B) of their declaration in the class  C) alphabetical  D) undefined\n\n**4.** The keyword `explicit` on a one-argument constructor\nA) makes it faster  B) prevents implicit conversions  C) makes it private  D) makes it virtual\n\n**5.** Which member functions can be called on a `const` object?\nA) any function  B) only const member functions  C) only static functions  D) only constructors\n\n**6.** The purpose of `override` is to\nA) make a function virtual  B) have the compiler check that a base-class virtual function is really overridden  C) prevent inheritance  D) hide the base function\n\n**7.** A pure virtual function is declared with\nA) `= 0`  B) `= delete`  C) `final`  D) `static`\n\n**8.** The vtable in a polymorphic class is\nA) one per object  B) one per class  C) one per program  D) one per member function\n\n**9.** The diamond problem in multiple inheritance is solved with\nA) friend classes  B) virtual inheritance  C) templates  D) private inheritance\n\n**10.** The Rule of Three concerns\nA) destructor, copy constructor, copy assignment  B) three constructors  C) three base classes  D) three template parameters\n\n**11.** `std::move(x)` does what?\nA) moves x immediately  B) casts x to an rvalue reference, allowing a move  C) deletes x  D) copies x\n\n**12.** `shared_ptr::use_count()` returns\nA) the size of the object  B) the number of shared_ptr owners  C) the number of weak_ptrs only  D) the address\n\n**13.** A template specialisation provides\nA) a special implementation for a particular type  B) a virtual function  C) a new base class  D) a namespace\n\n**14.** vector::reserve(n)\nA) changes the size to n  B) allocates capacity for n elements without changing the size  C) clears the vector  D) sorts n elements\n\n**15.** The average cost of a lookup in std::unordered_map is\nA) O(1)  B) O(log n)  C) O(n)  D) O(n log n)\n\n**16.** Which algorithm sorts a range?\nA) std::sort  B) std::find  C) std::accumulate  D) std::count\n\n**17.** Destructors should be\nA) allowed to throw  B) noexcept (never throw)  C) always virtual in every class  D) private\n\n**18.** A main drawback of the Singleton pattern is\nA) it uses too much memory  B) it introduces global state that hinders testing  C) it cannot be thread-safe  D) it needs templates\n\n**19.** The Observer pattern is used to\nA) create objects without naming classes  B) notify dependent objects when a subject changes  C) wrap an interface  D) copy objects\n\n**20.** The Open/Closed Principle says software entities should be\nA) open for modification, closed for use  B) open for extension, closed for modification  C) always final  D) always abstract\n\n## Section B: Short Questions (40 marks)\n\n**B1. (5)** Define encapsulation and give a short C++ example showing how it protects a class invariant.\n\n**B2. (5)** Predict the output and explain:\n```cpp\nstruct A { virtual string f() { return \"A\"; } string g() { return f() + \"!\"; } };\nstruct B : A { string f() override { return \"B\"; } };\nint main() { B b; A& r = b; cout << r.g() << \" \" << A(b).g(); }\n```\n\n**B3. (5)** The class below leaks and crashes when copied. Fix it by adding the destructor, copy constructor and copy assignment operator.\n```cpp\nclass Buffer {\npublic:\n    Buffer(size_t n) : n_(n), p_(new char[n]()) {}\nprivate:\n    size_t n_;\n    char* p_;\n};\n```\n\n**B4. (5)** Write `template <typename T> T clampTo(T v, T lo, T hi)` and give the results of `clampTo(15, 0, 10)`, `clampTo(-3, 0, 10)` and `clampTo(2.5, 0.0, 1.0)`.\n\n**B5. (5)** Using a `set<int>`, write code that prints the duplicates found when scanning `{3, 1, 4, 1, 5, 9, 2, 6, 5}` in order (print each value at the moment it is seen for the second time), and give the output.\n\n**B6. (5)** Predict the output:\n```cpp\ntry {\n    try { throw 1; }\n    catch (int) { cout << \"a \"; throw; }\n}\ncatch (...) { cout << \"b \"; }\ncout << \"c\";\n```\n\n**B7. (5)** Find and explain the bug, and fix it:\n```cpp\nint* raw = new int(5);\nshared_ptr<int> a(raw);\nshared_ptr<int> b(raw);\n```\n\n**B8. (5)** A class `ReportManager` reads data from a database, formats a report and e-mails it. Which SOLID principle does it violate? Propose a refactoring into suitable classes.\n\n## Section C: Long Questions (answer any four, 10 marks each)\n\n**C1. Payroll hierarchy.**\n(a) Write an abstract class Employee (name, base salary, virtual `pay() const`, virtual destructor) and derived classes Engineer (pay = base + 100 per project) and Manager (pay = base + 500 + 200 per direct report). [6]\n(b) Store Engineer(\"Ama\", 3000, 3 projects) and Manager(\"Kojo\", 4000, 2 reports) in a `vector<unique_ptr<Employee>>` and print each name and pay and the total payroll. [2]\n(c) Explain how you would add a Contractor type without modifying existing code and name the principle. [2]\n\n**C2. Copy and move semantics.** Study:\n```cpp\nstruct Buf {\n    Buf()                 { cout << \"ctor \"; }\n    Buf(const Buf&)       { cout << \"copy \"; }\n    Buf(Buf&&) noexcept   { cout << \"move \"; }\n};\nint main() {\n    Buf a;\n    Buf b = a;\n    Buf c = std::move(a);\n    vector<Buf> v;\n    v.reserve(2);\n    v.push_back(Buf());\n    v.push_back(b);\n}\n```\n(a) Predict the exact output and justify each token. [6]\n(b) Explain what changes if the move constructor is not declared noexcept and a vector reallocates. [2]\n(c) Explain the state of `a` after the move. [2]\n\n**C3. Observer and Strategy.**\n(a) Write an abstract Observer (virtual update(double)) and a Station that stores observers and notifies them in `set(double)`. [3]\n(b) Write a Display observer that prints \"T=\" and the value, and a Logger that counts updates and tracks the maximum. [3]\n(c) Predict the output of attaching a Display and a Logger and calling set(21.5), set(25), set(19), then printing the Logger's count and maximum. [2]\n(d) Explain how std::function could replace the Observer interface. [2]\n\n**C4. Generic programming.**\n(a) Write `template <typename T> const T& maxElement(const vector<T>& v)` that throws `invalid_argument` for an empty vector and uses only `operator<`. [3]\n(b) What does it return for `{3, 9, 2}` and `{\"pear\", \"apple\", \"zebra\"}`? [2]\n(c) Write a `Person` class (name, age) with the operator needed to find the oldest of Ama (30), Kojo (41), Esi (25), and give the result. [3]\n(d) Name two requirements this template places on its type T. [2]\n\n**C5. Design.** Design a small library system: a Library holds Books; Members borrow Books (at most 3 at a time); Librarians are Members who can also add and remove books; fines apply for late returns using a pluggable fine policy.\n(a) Give a class diagram (classes, key attributes and operations, relationships and multiplicities). [4]\n(b) Identify two design patterns that fit and where you would use them. [2]\n(c) Show how errors (for example borrowing a fourth book) are reported using exceptions. [2]\n(d) List four unit tests for the borrowing rules. [2]\n\n---\n\n# Marking Scheme and Complete Solutions\n\n## Section A answers\n1 **B** · 2 **A** · 3 **B** · 4 **B** · 5 **B** · 6 **B** · 7 **A** · 8 **B** · 9 **B** · 10 **A** · 11 **B** · 12 **B** · 13 **A** · 14 **B** · 15 **A** · 16 **A** · 17 **B** · 18 **B** · 19 **B** · 20 **B**\n\n## Section B solutions\n\n**B1.** **Encapsulation** bundles data with the functions that operate on it and hides the representation behind a public interface, so that the class itself guarantees its invariants. Example:\n```cpp\nclass Temperature {\npublic:\n    explicit Temperature(double celsius) { set(celsius); }\n    void set(double c) {\n        if (c < -273.15) throw invalid_argument(\"below absolute zero\");\n        celsius_ = c;\n    }\n    double celsius() const { return celsius_; }\nprivate:\n    double celsius_;          // invariant: celsius_ >= -273.15\n};\n```\nNo outside code can put the object in an invalid state because the only way to change celsius_ is through set. (2 marks definition, 2 example, 1 invariant.)\n\n**B2.** r is an A& referring to the B object, and g is not virtual but calls the virtual f through this, which dispatches dynamically: r.g() = \"B\" + \"!\" = \"B!\". `A(b)` slices b into a new A object, whose f is A::f: \"A!\". Output: **B! A!**.\n\n**B3.**\n```cpp\n~Buffer() { delete[] p_; }\nBuffer(const Buffer& o) : n_(o.n_), p_(new char[o.n_]) { std::copy(o.p_, o.p_ + n_, p_); }\nBuffer& operator=(const Buffer& o) {\n    if (this != &o) {\n        char* q = new char[o.n_];\n        std::copy(o.p_, o.p_ + o.n_, q);\n        delete[] p_;\n        p_ = q;\n        n_ = o.n_;\n    }\n    return *this;\n}\n```\n(Better: replace the raw array by `std::vector<char>` and apply the Rule of Zero.)\n\n**B4.**\n```cpp\ntemplate <typename T>\nT clampTo(T v, T lo, T hi) { return v < lo ? lo : (hi < v ? hi : v); }\n```\nclampTo(15, 0, 10) = **10**; clampTo(-3, 0, 10) = **0**; clampTo(2.5, 0.0, 1.0) = **1** (printed as 1).\n\n**B5.**\n```cpp\nvector<int> data = {3, 1, 4, 1, 5, 9, 2, 6, 5};\nset<int> seen;\nfor (int x : data) {\n    if (!seen.insert(x).second) cout << x << \" \";     // insert returns false if already present\n}\n```\nThe second 1 is met first and then the second 5: output **1 5 **.\n\n**B6.** The inner catch prints \"a \", then `throw;` rethrows the same exception, which the outer catch(...) handles: \"b \". Then \"c\". Output: **a b c**.\n\n**B7.** Both shared_ptrs are constructed from the same raw pointer, so they have **independent control blocks** (each believes it is the sole owner). When both are destroyed, `delete raw` runs twice: **double delete, undefined behaviour**. Fix: create the pointer once with `auto a = make_shared<int>(5); auto b = a;` (sharing one control block), or `shared_ptr<int> b = a;`.\n\n**B8.** It violates the **Single Responsibility Principle** (it has three reasons to change: the data source, the report format and the delivery channel). Refactor into `ReportRepository` (data access, ideally an interface `IReportSource`), `ReportFormatter` (formatting, possibly several strategies) and `ReportSender` (an interface `ISender` with an `EmailSender`), plus a small coordinating class that receives them through its constructor (dependency inversion), which also makes each part testable with a fake.\n\n## Section C solutions\n\n**C1.**\n(a)\n```cpp\nclass Employee {\npublic:\n    Employee(string name, double base) : base_(base), name_(std::move(name)) {}   // same order as the declarations below\n    virtual double pay() const = 0;\n    virtual ~Employee() = default;\n    const string& name() const { return name_; }\nprotected:\n    double base_;\nprivate:\n    string name_;\n};\nclass Engineer : public Employee {\npublic:\n    Engineer(string n, double b, int projects) : Employee(std::move(n), b), projects_(projects) {}\n    double pay() const override { return base_ + 100 * projects_; }\nprivate:\n    int projects_;\n};\nclass Manager : public Employee {\npublic:\n    Manager(string n, double b, int reports) : Employee(std::move(n), b), reports_(reports) {}\n    double pay() const override { return base_ + 500 + 200 * reports_; }\nprivate:\n    int reports_;\n};\n```\n(b)\n```cpp\nvector<unique_ptr<Employee>> staff;\nstaff.push_back(make_unique<Engineer>(\"Ama\", 3000, 3));\nstaff.push_back(make_unique<Manager>(\"Kojo\", 4000, 2));\ndouble total = 0;\nfor (const auto& e : staff) {\n    cout << e->name() << \" \" << e->pay() << \"\\n\";\n    total += e->pay();\n}\ncout << \"total \" << total << \"\\n\";\n```\nOutput: **Ama 3300**, **Kojo 4900**, **total 8200** (Ama: 3000 + 300; Kojo: 4000 + 500 + 400).\n(c) Derive `Contractor` from Employee and override pay (for example hourly rate times hours); existing classes and the payroll loop are unchanged because they depend only on the Employee interface: the **Open/Closed Principle** (and dependency inversion).\n\n**C2.**\n(a) `Buf a;` prints **ctor**. `Buf b = a;` copy-constructs from an lvalue: **copy**. `Buf c = std::move(a);` casts a to an rvalue, selecting the move constructor: **move**. `v.reserve(2)` allocates but constructs nothing. `v.push_back(Buf())` constructs a temporary (**ctor**) and then moves it into the vector (**move**), since the argument is an rvalue. `v.push_back(b)` copies the lvalue b into the vector: **copy** (no reallocation, because the capacity is 2). Output: **ctor copy move ctor move copy **.\n(b) If the move constructor can throw, `std::vector` cannot give its strong exception guarantee when it relocates elements on reallocation, so it **copies** the existing elements instead of moving them (it uses `std::move_if_noexcept`); performance suffers. Mark move operations `noexcept`.\n(c) The moved-from object a is in a **valid but unspecified state** (for a typical resource-owning class: empty, with a null pointer). You may safely destroy it or assign to it, but must not rely on its value.\n\n**C3.**\n(a)\n```cpp\nstruct Observer {\n    virtual void update(double t) = 0;\n    virtual ~Observer() = default;\n};\nclass Station {\npublic:\n    void attach(Observer* o) { obs_.push_back(o); }\n    void set(double t) { for (auto* o : obs_) o->update(t); }\nprivate:\n    vector<Observer*> obs_;\n};\n```\n(b)\n```cpp\nstruct Display : Observer {\n    void update(double t) override { cout << \"T=\" << t << \" \"; }\n};\nstruct Logger : Observer {\n    int n = 0;\n    double max = -1e9;\n    void update(double t) override { ++n; if (t > max) max = t; }\n};\n```\n(c)\n```cpp\nStation s; Display d; Logger l;\ns.attach(&d); s.attach(&l);\ns.set(21.5); s.set(25); s.set(19);\ncout << \"| \" << l.n << \" \" << l.max;\n```\nEach set notifies both observers in attachment order. Output: **T=21.5 T=25 T=19 | 3 25**.\n(d) Replace the Observer interface by `vector<function<void(double)>>` and attach lambdas: `s.subscribe([](double t){ cout << \"T=\" << t << \" \"; });`. Observers then need no common base class, and state can be captured by the lambda.\n\n**C4.**\n(a)\n```cpp\ntemplate <typename T>\nconst T& maxElement(const vector<T>& v) {\n    if (v.empty()) throw invalid_argument(\"empty vector\");\n    const T* best = &v[0];\n    for (const T& x : v)\n        if (*best < x) best = &x;\n    return *best;\n}\n```\n(b) For {3, 9, 2} it returns **9**; for the strings it returns **\"zebra\"** (lexicographic comparison).\n(c)\n```cpp\nclass Person {\npublic:\n    Person(string n, int a) : name_(std::move(n)), age_(a) {}\n    bool operator<(const Person& o) const { return age_ < o.age_; }\n    const string& name() const { return name_; }\nprivate:\n    string name_;\n    int age_;\n};\nvector<Person> p = {{\"Ama\", 30}, {\"Kojo\", 41}, {\"Esi\", 25}};\ncout << maxElement(p).name();\n```\nThe result is **Kojo** (age 41).\n(d) T must be **comparable with `operator<`** (a strict weak ordering) and, since the function returns a const reference to an element, the vector must outlive the use of the result; T need not be default-constructible or copyable (the function does not copy elements). (Accept: \"T must support <\" and \"T must be copy/reference-safe\".)\n\n**C5.**\n(a) Classes: **Library** (books: collection of Book, members; addBook, removeBook, findBook, checkOut, returnBook); **Book** (isbn, title, available; borrowedBy pointer); **Member** (id, name, borrowed: up to 3 Books; borrow, giveBack, fines); **Librarian** inherits publicly from Member (adds addBook and removeBook through the Library); **FinePolicy** (abstract: `virtual double fine(int daysLate) const = 0`) with concrete **FlatFinePolicy** and **PerDayFinePolicy**. Relationships: Library ◆── 0..* Book (composition: books belong to the library); Library ◇── 0..* Member (aggregation); Member 0..1 ── 0..3 Book (\"borrows\": a member has at most 3 loans and a book at most one borrower); Librarian ──▷ Member (inheritance); Library ──> FinePolicy (association, injected).\n(b) **Strategy** for the fine policy (swap flat or per-day fines without changing the Library); **Factory** (create books or members from a file), **Observer** (notify members when a reserved book becomes available) or **Singleton** (not recommended) are also acceptable.\n(c)\n```cpp\nstruct LoanLimitExceeded : runtime_error {\n    LoanLimitExceeded() : runtime_error(\"member already has 3 books\") {}\n};\nvoid Member::borrow(Book& b) {\n    if (borrowed_.size() >= 3) throw LoanLimitExceeded();\n    if (!b.available()) throw runtime_error(\"book not available\");\n    borrowed_.push_back(&b);\n    b.setBorrower(this);\n}\n```\nThe caller catches the specific exception and reports it to the user.\n(d) Tests: (1) a member can borrow up to 3 books; (2) borrowing a 4th book throws LoanLimitExceeded and leaves the loans unchanged; (3) a book already on loan cannot be borrowed by another member; (4) returning a book makes it available again and removes it from the member's list (and, with the fine policy injected as a test double, a late return of 5 days produces the expected fine).\n","notes":"# Object-Oriented Programming (C++): Complete Lecture Notes\n\nThis course teaches object-oriented design and programming in **C++17**. Object-oriented programming (OOP) organises software as interacting **objects** that combine data and the operations on it. Its four pillars are **encapsulation**, **abstraction**, **inheritance** and **polymorphism**. C++ adds control over memory and performance, so it is also the language in which to understand object lifetime, copying and resource management. Compile with `g++ -std=c++17 -Wall -Wextra -g file.cpp`.\n\n## Chapter 1. From C to C++\n\n### 1.1 A first program\n```cpp\n#include <iostream>\n#include <string>\n\nint main() {\n    std::string name = \"Ama\";\n    std::cout << \"Hello, \" << name << \"!\\n\";\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n`std::cout` is an output stream, `<<` the insertion operator, `std::cin >> x` reads input. `std::` is the standard **namespace**; `using namespace std;` is acceptable in small examples but not in headers.\n\n### 1.2 Improvements over C\n- **Types:** `bool`, `std::string` (instead of char arrays), `auto` type deduction (`auto x = 3.5;` is a double), range-for (`for (int v : vec)`), `nullptr` instead of NULL, `enum class` (scoped enumerations).\n- **References:** `int& r = x;` is an alias for x (it must be initialised and cannot be re-seated). Pass large objects by `const T&` to avoid copying; use `T&` for output parameters. A reference is safer than a pointer when null is not meaningful.\n- **const:** `const int x = 5;`, `const T&` parameters, `const` member functions. `constexpr` for compile-time constants.\n- **Dynamic memory:** `new` and `delete` (and `new[]`/`delete[]`) replace malloc/free but should be avoided in favour of containers and smart pointers (Chapter 4).\n- **Function overloading and default arguments:** `void f(int)`, `void f(double)`, `void g(int a, int b = 10)`.\n- **Standard library:** `std::vector`, `std::string`, `std::map`, algorithms, streams.\n\n## Chapter 2. Classes and Encapsulation\n\n### 2.1 Defining a class\n```cpp\nclass BankAccount {\npublic:\n    BankAccount(std::string owner, double balance = 0.0)\n        : owner_(std::move(owner)), balance_(balance) {}\n    void deposit(double amount) {\n        if (amount <= 0) throw std::invalid_argument(\"amount must be positive\");\n        balance_ += amount;\n    }\n    bool withdraw(double amount) {\n        if (amount <= 0 || amount > balance_) return false;\n        balance_ -= amount;\n        return true;\n    }\n    double balance() const { return balance_; }      // const: does not modify the object\n    const std::string& owner() const { return owner_; }\nprivate:\n    std::string owner_;\n    double balance_;\n};\n```\n- **Encapsulation:** data members are `private`; the public interface maintains the **class invariant** (here balance_ is never negative). `struct` is a class whose members are public by default.\n- **Constructors** initialise objects. Use the **member initialiser list** (`: owner_(...), balance_(...)`): members are initialised in the order they are *declared*, not the order in the list. A **default constructor** takes no arguments; if you define any constructor, the compiler no longer generates a default one. Mark single-argument constructors `explicit` to prevent unwanted implicit conversions. **Delegating constructors** call another constructor of the same class. Prefer uniform initialisation `BankAccount a{\"Kofi\", 50.0};`.\n- **Destructor** `~ClassName()` runs when an object's lifetime ends (leaving scope, delete, container destruction); it releases resources.\n- **this** is a pointer to the current object (`this->balance_`). Methods can return `*this` to allow chaining.\n- **static members** belong to the class, not to objects: `static int count;` (defined once outside the class, or `inline static` in C++17), `static` member functions have no `this`.\n- **friend** functions and classes can access private members; use sparingly.\n\n### 2.2 Object lifetime and the order of construction and destruction\n- Objects are constructed in the order of their definition and destroyed in the **reverse order**.\n- When constructing an object, **base classes are constructed first**, then **data members in declaration order**, then the constructor body runs. Destruction runs the destructor body, then members in reverse order, then the base classes.\n```cpp\nstruct A { A() { std::cout << \"A+ \"; } ~A() { std::cout << \"A- \"; } };\nstruct B : A { B() { std::cout << \"B+ \"; } ~B() { std::cout << \"B- \"; } };\nstruct C { A a; B b; C() { std::cout << \"C+ \"; } ~C() { std::cout << \"C- \"; } };\nint main() { C c; std::cout << \"| \"; }\n```\nThis prints `A+ A+ B+ C+ | C- B- A- A- ` (a is built first, then b builds its base A and then B, then C's body; destruction reverses everything).\n- **Storage durations:** automatic (stack, destroyed at the end of scope), dynamic (heap, until deleted), static (whole program), thread-local.\n\n### 2.3 Const correctness\nMark every member function that does not change the object `const`. A const object can only call const member functions. `mutable` members can change even in const functions (for example caches). Return `const T&` for large read-only results.\n\n## Chapter 3. Operator Overloading and Value Semantics\n\n### 3.1 Overloading\nOperators are functions with special names. Overload to make user types natural, never to surprise.\n```cpp\nclass Vec2 {\npublic:\n    Vec2(double x = 0, double y = 0) : x_(x), y_(y) {}\n    Vec2 operator+(const Vec2& o) const { return Vec2(x_ + o.x_, y_ + o.y_); }\n    Vec2& operator+=(const Vec2& o) { x_ += o.x_; y_ += o.y_; return *this; }\n    double dot(const Vec2& o) const { return x_ * o.x_ + y_ * o.y_; }\n    bool operator==(const Vec2& o) const { return x_ == o.x_ && y_ == o.y_; }\n    friend std::ostream& operator<<(std::ostream& os, const Vec2& v) {\n        return os << \"(\" << v.x_ << \", \" << v.y_ << \")\";\n    }\nprivate:\n    double x_, y_;\n};\n```\n`Vec2(1,2) + Vec2(3,4)` prints `(4, 6)` and `dot` gives 11. Guidelines: keep the usual meaning; implement `+=` and build `+` from it; `operator<<` is a non-member (friend); `operator[]`, `operator()` (function objects), `operator=` and the comparison operators (C++20 adds `<=>`) are common; do not overload `&&`, `||`, `,`. Conversion operators and `explicit`.\n\n### 3.2 Copying: the Rule of Three, Five and Zero\nWhen a class owns a resource (memory, a file handle) the compiler-generated copy only copies pointers (a **shallow copy**), leading to double deletion.\n```cpp\nclass IntArray {\npublic:\n    explicit IntArray(size_t n) : n_(n), data_(new int[n]()) {}\n    IntArray(const IntArray& o) : n_(o.n_), data_(new int[o.n_]) {       // copy constructor\n        std::copy(o.data_, o.data_ + n_, data_);\n    }\n    IntArray& operator=(const IntArray& o) {                              // copy assignment\n        if (this != &o) {\n            int* p = new int[o.n_];\n            std::copy(o.data_, o.data_ + o.n_, p);\n            delete[] data_;\n            data_ = p; n_ = o.n_;\n        }\n        return *this;\n    }\n    ~IntArray() { delete[] data_; }                                        // destructor\n    int& operator[](size_t i) { return data_[i]; }\n    size_t size() const { return n_; }\nprivate:\n    size_t n_;\n    int* data_;\n};\n```\n- **Rule of Three:** if you need a custom destructor, copy constructor or copy assignment operator, you probably need all three.\n- **Rule of Five** (C++11): add the **move constructor** and **move assignment**, which *steal* the resources from a temporary (an rvalue): `IntArray(IntArray&& o) noexcept : n_(o.n_), data_(o.data_) { o.n_ = 0; o.data_ = nullptr; }`. Moves make returning large objects and growing vectors cheap. `std::move(x)` casts to an rvalue; the moved-from object must remain valid but unspecified.\n- **Rule of Zero:** prefer classes that own resources through members that manage themselves (`std::vector`, `std::string`, smart pointers) so that the compiler-generated special functions are correct. Use `= default` and `= delete` to state intent (`Class(const Class&) = delete;` makes a type non-copyable).\n- **Copy elision:** returning a local by value usually constructs it in place (guaranteed for prvalues in C++17).\n\n## Chapter 4. Resource Management (RAII) and Smart Pointers\n\n**RAII (Resource Acquisition Is Initialisation):** acquire a resource in a constructor and release it in the destructor, so cleanup is automatic, even when exceptions occur. Examples: `std::vector`, `std::string`, `std::fstream`, `std::lock_guard`, smart pointers.\n- **`std::unique_ptr<T>`:** sole ownership, zero overhead, move-only. `auto p = std::make_unique<Widget>(args);` After `auto q = std::move(p);` p is null.\n- **`std::shared_ptr<T>`:** shared ownership by reference counting; `std::make_shared<T>(args)`; `use_count()` gives the number of owners. A cycle of shared_ptrs leaks; break it with **`std::weak_ptr`** (non-owning observer).\n- Rules: never `delete` what a smart pointer owns; use raw pointers and references only for non-owning access; prefer `make_unique` and `make_shared` to `new`.\n```cpp\nauto a = std::make_shared<int>(5);\nauto b = a;                              // use_count = 2\n{ auto c = a; std::cout << a.use_count(); }   // prints 3, then c is destroyed\nstd::cout << a.use_count();              // prints 2\n```\n\n## Chapter 5. Inheritance and Polymorphism\n\n### 5.1 Inheritance\n`class Derived : public Base { ... };` models an **is-a** relationship: a Derived can be used wherever a Base is expected (Liskov substitution principle). Derived inherits the public and protected members of Base, extends or **overrides** behaviour. `protected` members are visible to derived classes. Use `public` inheritance for is-a; prefer composition otherwise. A derived constructor passes arguments to the base: `Derived(int a, int b) : Base(a), b_(b) {}`.\n\n### 5.2 Virtual functions and dynamic dispatch\n```cpp\nstruct Base {\n    void f()         { std::cout << \"Base::f \"; }     // non-virtual: chosen at compile time\n    virtual void g() { std::cout << \"Base::g \"; }     // virtual: chosen at run time\n    virtual ~Base() = default;\n};\nstruct Der : Base {\n    void f()          { std::cout << \"Der::f \"; }     // hides Base::f\n    void g() override { std::cout << \"Der::g \"; }\n};\nint main() {\n    Der d; Base* p = &d;\n    p->f(); p->g(); d.f();\n    Base b = d; b.g();                                // slicing\n}\n```\nOutput: `Base::f Der::g Der::f Base::g `. A call through a **pointer or reference to Base** to a **virtual** function runs the version of the object's real (dynamic) type. Non-virtual functions are resolved by the static type. **Object slicing:** assigning a Derived to a Base *object* copies only the Base part, losing the derived behaviour; use pointers or references.\n- **`override`** makes the compiler check that you really override a virtual function; **`final`** forbids further overriding.\n- **Virtual destructors:** a base class with virtual functions must have a **virtual destructor**, otherwise `delete basePtr;` on a Derived object is undefined behaviour (the derived destructor does not run).\n- **Abstract classes** have a pure virtual function (`virtual double area() const = 0;`) and cannot be instantiated; they define an **interface**. A class implementing all pure virtuals is concrete.\n- **How it works:** each polymorphic object has a hidden pointer to a **vtable** (a table of function pointers per class); a virtual call costs one extra indirection. Do not call virtual functions from constructors or destructors (the dynamic type is the class being constructed).\n```cpp\nstruct Shape {\n    virtual double area() const = 0;\n    virtual std::string name() const { return \"shape\"; }\n    virtual ~Shape() = default;\n};\nstruct Rect : Shape {\n    Rect(double w, double h) : w_(w), h_(h) {}\n    double area() const override { return w_ * h_; }\n    std::string name() const override { return \"rect\"; }\n    double w_, h_;\n};\nstd::vector<std::unique_ptr<Shape>> shapes;\nshapes.push_back(std::make_unique<Rect>(3, 4));\ndouble total = 0;\nfor (const auto& s : shapes) total += s->area();        // polymorphic call\n```\n\n### 5.3 Multiple inheritance and interfaces\nA class can inherit from several bases. The **diamond problem** (two paths to the same base) is solved with `virtual` inheritance. Prefer pure-virtual interface classes (no data) for multiple inheritance of *capabilities* (as in Java interfaces). Also: **static vs dynamic type**, `dynamic_cast` for safe down-casts (needs a polymorphic base), `typeid`, and the cost of RTTI.\n\n### 5.4 Composition versus inheritance\nPrefer **composition** (\"has-a\") to inheritance (\"is-a\") when you only need to reuse implementation: a `Car` *has* an `Engine`. Inheritance couples the derived class to the base class's implementation. \"Favour composition over inheritance\" is a core design guideline.\n\n## Chapter 6. Object-Oriented Design\n\n### 6.1 Principles\n- **SOLID:** **S**ingle responsibility (a class has one reason to change); **O**pen/closed (open for extension, closed for modification: add new subclasses, do not edit working code); **L**iskov substitution (subtypes must honour the base contract; a Square that inherits from Rectangle but breaks \"set width independently\" violates it); **I**nterface segregation (many small interfaces); **D**ependency inversion (depend on abstractions, not concrete classes).\n- **Low coupling, high cohesion.** Program to an interface, not an implementation. **Law of Demeter** (talk only to your immediate collaborators). **DRY** (do not repeat yourself).\n\n### 6.2 UML class diagrams\nA class box has three compartments (name, attributes, operations) with visibility (+ public, - private, # protected). Relationships: **association** (a line), **aggregation** (open diamond: has-a, parts can exist independently), **composition** (filled diamond: strong ownership, parts die with the whole), **inheritance** (hollow triangle arrow), **dependency** (dashed arrow), **realisation** (dashed line with hollow triangle: implements an interface). Multiplicities (1, 0..*, 1..*). Sequence diagrams show interactions over time.\n\n### 6.3 Design patterns\nReusable solutions to recurring design problems (the \"Gang of Four\" catalogue).\n- **Singleton** (one instance): `static Config& instance() { static Config c; return c; }` (thread-safe initialisation in C++11). Use sparingly: it is global state.\n- **Factory (method):** create objects without naming concrete classes: `std::unique_ptr<Shape> make(const std::string& kind)` returns Rect or Circle.\n- **Strategy:** a family of interchangeable algorithms behind an interface or `std::function` (a sort comparator, a pricing rule).\n- **Observer:** a subject notifies registered observers of changes (event handlers, GUI signals).\n```cpp\nclass Subject {\npublic:\n    void subscribe(std::function<void(int)> f) { observers_.push_back(std::move(f)); }\n    void set(int v) { value_ = v; for (auto& o : observers_) o(value_); }\nprivate:\n    std::vector<std::function<void(int)>> observers_;\n    int value_ = 0;\n};\n```\n- **Decorator** (wrap an object to add behaviour: buffered stream over a file stream), **Adapter** (convert an interface), **Command** (encapsulate a request, enabling undo), **Iterator** (traverse a container, built into the STL), **Template method**, **State**, **Composite** (tree of objects with a uniform interface), **Facade**, **RAII**.\nPatterns are tools, not goals. A pattern misapplied adds complexity.\n\n## Chapter 7. Templates and Generic Programming\n\n### 7.1 Function and class templates\n```cpp\ntemplate <typename T>\nT maxOf(T a, T b) { return (a > b) ? a : b; }\n\ntemplate <typename T>\nclass Stack {\npublic:\n    void push(const T& v) { items_.push_back(v); }\n    T pop() {\n        if (items_.empty()) throw std::out_of_range(\"empty stack\");\n        T v = std::move(items_.back());\n        items_.pop_back();\n        return v;\n    }\n    bool empty() const { return items_.empty(); }\n    size_t size() const { return items_.size(); }\nprivate:\n    std::vector<T> items_;\n};\n```\n`maxOf(3, 7)` is 7; `maxOf(2.5, 1.5)` is 2.5; `maxOf(std::string(\"apple\"), std::string(\"pear\"))` is \"pear\". `maxOf(\"a\", \"b\")` compares pointers (a classic bug): T is deduced as `const char*`. Templates are instantiated at compile time for each type used (no run-time cost; code lives in headers; errors can be long). **Non-type parameters** (`template <typename T, size_t N> class Array`), **default arguments**, **specialisation** (`template <> class Stack<bool>`), **variadic templates**, and **concepts** (C++20) constraining T. **Static (compile-time) polymorphism** via templates versus **dynamic polymorphism** via virtual functions.\n\n### 7.2 The Standard Template Library\n- **Containers:** `vector` (dynamic array, contiguous, amortised O(1) push_back, O(1) indexing), `deque`, `list` (doubly linked), `array`, `string`; ordered `set`/`map` (balanced tree, O(log n)), `unordered_set`/`unordered_map` (hash table, average O(1)); adaptors `stack`, `queue`, `priority_queue` (heap).\n- **Iterators** generalise pointers: `begin()`, `end()` (one past the last), `*it`, `++it`. Invalidation: inserting into a vector may invalidate iterators.\n- **Algorithms** (`<algorithm>`, `<numeric>`): `sort`, `find`, `count_if`, `transform`, `accumulate`, `copy`, `remove_if`, `reverse`, `binary_search`, `min_element`. The **erase-remove idiom**: `v.erase(std::remove_if(v.begin(), v.end(), pred), v.end());`.\n```cpp\nstd::vector<int> v = {5, 2, 8, 1};\nstd::sort(v.begin(), v.end());                          // 1 2 5 8\nint sum = std::accumulate(v.begin(), v.end(), 0);       // 16\nv.erase(std::remove_if(v.begin(), v.end(),\n        [](int x) { return x % 2 == 0; }), v.end());    // keeps 1 5\n```\n- **Lambdas:** `[capture](params) -> ret { body }`; `[&]` captures by reference, `[=]` by value; `std::function<R(Args...)>` stores any callable. Lambdas are the usual way to pass behaviour to algorithms and to implement strategies and callbacks.\n- Choose containers by operations and complexity; prefer `vector` unless you have a reason; use `reserve` when the size is known.\n\n## Chapter 8. Exceptions and Error Handling\n\n- `throw`, `try`, `catch`. Exceptions propagate up the call stack, performing **stack unwinding** (destructors of local objects run, which is why RAII is essential). Catch by `const&`: `catch (const std::exception& e) { std::cerr << e.what(); }`. The standard hierarchy: `std::exception` → `std::logic_error` (`invalid_argument`, `out_of_range`), `std::runtime_error`, `bad_alloc`.\n- **Exception safety guarantees:** basic (no leaks, valid state), strong (commit or roll back), nothrow (`noexcept`, required of destructors and moves). Never let an exception escape a destructor.\n- Use exceptions for exceptional conditions, not normal control flow. Alternatives: return codes, `std::optional<T>`, `std::variant`, error objects. Validate preconditions with `assert` in debug builds.\n```cpp\ntry {\n    Stack<int> s;\n    s.push(1);\n    s.pop();\n    s.pop();                                  // throws std::out_of_range\n} catch (const std::out_of_range& e) {\n    std::cout << \"caught: \" << e.what();      // caught: empty stack\n}\n```\n\n## Chapter 9. Testing, Documentation and Good Practice\n- **Unit testing** with a framework (GoogleTest, Catch2, doctest): test each class's public interface, including invariants, edge cases and exceptions. Use dependency injection (pass collaborators through constructors or interfaces) to make classes testable with test doubles (mocks, fakes).\n- **Tools:** compiler warnings (`-Wall -Wextra`), sanitisers (`-fsanitize=address,undefined`), valgrind, clang-tidy, clang-format, version control, and a build system (CMake).\n- **Style:** short functions, meaningful names, consistent formatting, comments that explain *why*, header files with include guards or `#pragma once`, keep interfaces small.\n- **Refactoring:** improve structure without changing behaviour, protected by tests (extract method, replace conditional with polymorphism, introduce interface).\n\n## Chapter 10. Common Mistakes Checklist\n1. Forgetting a virtual destructor in a polymorphic base class.\n2. Slicing: passing or storing polymorphic objects by value.\n3. Shallow copies of classes that own raw pointers (violating the Rule of Three/Five).\n4. Using `new` without a matching `delete`; mixing `new[]` with `delete`.\n5. Initialiser-list order differing from the declaration order (members initialise in declaration order).\n6. Returning a reference or pointer to a local variable.\n7. Calling virtual functions in constructors/destructors expecting dynamic dispatch.\n8. Missing `const` on member functions or parameters; modifying a container while iterating.\n9. Using inheritance for code reuse where composition fits; deep hierarchies.\n10. Catching exceptions by value (slicing) or swallowing them silently.\n","plan":"# Object-Oriented Programming (C++): 14-Week Study Plan and Resources\n\nTime budget: 9 to 12 hours per week. As with every programming course, **type and run code every day**. Each week: read the chapter (2 h), do the assignment problems (4 to 5 h), build or extend one project (3 h), review with the app's quizzes (1 h). Set up g++ (or clang++), GDB or LLDB, valgrind or sanitisers (-fsanitize=address,undefined), CMake and a unit-test library (Catch2 or GoogleTest).\n\n## Textbooks\n- Bjarne Stroustrup, *A Tour of C++* (concise) and *Programming: Principles and Practice Using C++*.\n- Stanley Lippman, Josée Lajoie and Barbara Moo, *C++ Primer*. Thorough, with exercises.\n- Scott Meyers, *Effective Modern C++* (read after the course basics) and *Effective C++*.\n- Gamma, Helm, Johnson and Vlissides, *Design Patterns: Elements of Reusable Object-Oriented Software*; Freeman and Robson, *Head First Design Patterns* (gentler).\n- Robert C. Martin, *Clean Code* and *Clean Architecture* for design principles.\n- The C++ Core Guidelines (free online) and cppreference.com.\n\n## Free courses and tools\n- learncpp.com (a complete free tutorial).\n- CS106B/CS106L (Stanford) and MIT OpenCourseWare 6.096 Introduction to C++.\n- Compiler Explorer (godbolt.org) to see what the compiler generates (vtables, inlining).\n- Exercism C++ track, Advent of Code and Codeforces for practice.\n\n## Week by week\n\n**Week 1: From C to C++ (Ch 1).** Re-write two earlier C programs in C++ using string, vector and references. Do: Assignment 1 Q6.\n\n**Week 2: Classes and encapsulation (Ch 2.1).** Do: Assignment 1 Q1. Project: a BankAccount or Inventory class with unit tests.\n\n**Week 3: Object lifetime, const correctness, static members (Ch 2.2, 2.3).** Do: Assignment 1 Q2, Q5. Use a Tracer class to print constructor and destructor calls in every experiment.\n\n**Week 4: Operator overloading (Ch 3.1).** Do: Assignment 1 Q4. Sit the **Class Test** at the end of the week.\n\n**Week 5: Copying, the Rule of Three/Five/Zero, move semantics (Ch 3.2).** Do: Assignment 1 Q3. Write a String or Matrix class that owns memory, test it with the address sanitiser.\n\n**Week 6: RAII and smart pointers (Ch 4).** Do: Assignment 3 Q8. Refactor your earlier code to remove all raw new and delete.\n\n**Week 7: Inheritance and virtual functions (Ch 5.1, 5.2).** Do: Assignment 2 Q1, Q3, Q4, Q8.\n\n**Week 8: Abstract classes, interfaces, composition (Ch 5.2 to 5.4).** Do: Assignment 2 Q2, Q5. Mock midterm: first half of the Final Exam.\n\n**Week 9: OO design principles and UML (Ch 6.1, 6.2).** Do: Assignment 2 Q6, Q7. Draw a class diagram for your project.\n\n**Week 10: Design patterns I (Ch 6.3).** Do: Assignment 3 Q6. Implement Strategy, Observer and Factory in one small application.\n\n**Week 11: Templates (Ch 7.1).** Do: Assignment 3 Q1, Q2. Write a generic Stack and a generic function with constraints.\n\n**Week 12: The STL (Ch 7.2).** Do: Assignment 3 Q3, Q4, Q5. Solve ten problems using only STL containers and algorithms.\n\n**Week 13: Exceptions and testing (Ch 8, 9).** Do: Assignment 3 Q7. Add exception handling and a unit-test suite (Catch2) to your project.\n\n**Week 14: Capstone and revision.** Finish a capstone project (for example a library management system, a text-based game, a bank simulator or a shape-drawing program) with a class diagram, at least one pattern, tests and no memory errors. Redo missed Class Test questions and sit the **Final Exam** under exam conditions (3 hours, no computer).\n\n## Milestone checklist\n- [ ] I can design a class with a clear invariant, constructors, const-correct methods and a destructor when needed.\n- [ ] I can predict the order of construction, copying and destruction in any program.\n- [ ] I can implement the Rule of Three/Five and explain move semantics.\n- [ ] I can use unique_ptr and shared_ptr instead of raw owning pointers.\n- [ ] I can write class hierarchies with virtual functions, abstract interfaces and virtual destructors, and avoid slicing.\n- [ ] I can justify composition versus inheritance and apply the SOLID principles.\n- [ ] I can write class and function templates and use STL containers, iterators and algorithms with lambdas.\n- [ ] I can apply at least four design patterns appropriately.\n- [ ] I can write exception-safe code and unit tests.\n- [ ] I scored at least 70 percent on the Final Exam with no computer.\n\n## Study habits that work\n1. Instrument your classes: print in every constructor, destructor, copy and move, to see the rules at work.\n2. Predict, then run. Wrong predictions are your best teachers.\n3. Always build with warnings and sanitisers. Treat warnings as errors.\n4. Design on paper (CRC cards or UML) before coding larger programs: ask what each class knows, does and collaborates with.\n5. Read the C++ Core Guidelines a few rules at a time and apply them to your code.\n","test":"# Object-Oriented Programming (C++): Class Test\n\nDuration: 60 minutes. No computer. Total: 60 marks. Section A: 20 multiple-choice questions (1 mark each). Section B: 4 problems (10 marks each). Assume `#include` lines for the standard headers and `using namespace std;` in code fragments.\n\n## Section A: Multiple choice (20 marks)\n\n**1.** Hiding the data of a class behind a public interface is called\nA) inheritance  B) encapsulation  C) overloading  D) templating\n\n**2.** Private members of a class can be accessed\nA) by any function  B) only by derived classes  C) by the class's own member functions (and friends)  D) only in main\n\n**3.** A constructor is called\nA) when an object is created  B) when an object is destroyed  C) when a function returns  D) never automatically\n\n**4.** Local objects are destroyed in\nA) the same order as construction  B) the reverse order of construction  C) random order  D) alphabetical order\n\n**5.** The keyword `virtual` on a member function enables\nA) faster calls  B) dynamic dispatch (run-time polymorphism)  C) private access  D) static linking\n\n**6.** An abstract class is one that\nA) has no data members  B) has at least one pure virtual function  C) cannot have a constructor  D) has only static members\n\n**7.** `class Dog : public Animal` expresses\nA) a has-a relationship  B) an is-a relationship  C) a uses-a relationship  D) no relationship\n\n**8.** The copy constructor is invoked when\nA) an object is passed by value to a function  B) an object is passed by reference  C) a pointer is passed  D) a static is created\n\n**9.** A `const` member function\nA) cannot be called on a non-const object  B) promises not to modify the object's members  C) is always inline  D) must be static\n\n**10.** Compared with a pointer, a reference\nA) can be null  B) can be re-seated to another object  C) must be initialised and cannot be re-seated  D) takes more memory\n\n**11.** Memory allocated with `new[]` must be released with\nA) delete  B) free  C) delete[]  D) release\n\n**12.** Which smart pointer expresses sole ownership?\nA) shared_ptr  B) unique_ptr  C) weak_ptr  D) auto_ptr only\n\n**13.** Templates provide\nA) run-time polymorphism  B) generic programming resolved at compile time  C) garbage collection  D) multiple inheritance\n\n**14.** The amortised complexity of vector::push_back is\nA) O(n)  B) O(log n)  C) O(1)  D) O(n^2)\n\n**15.** std::map stores its keys\nA) in insertion order  B) in sorted order  C) in random order  D) in reverse order\n\n**16.** The recommended way to catch an exception is\nA) by value  B) by const reference  C) by raw pointer  D) with catch(...) only\n\n**17.** A virtual destructor is required in a base class when\nA) the class has no data  B) objects are deleted through base-class pointers  C) the class is final  D) the class is a template\n\n**18.** The stream insertion operator `operator<<` for a class is normally\nA) a non-member (often friend) function  B) a static member  C) a constructor  D) a virtual function\n\n**19.** A static data member is\nA) copied into each object  B) shared by all objects of the class  C) always constant  D) only usable in main\n\n**20.** Assigning a derived-class object to a base-class object (by value) causes\nA) a compile error always  B) slicing: the derived part is lost  C) a run-time exception  D) nothing\n\n## Section B: Problems (40 marks)\n\n**B1. (10 marks)** Write a class Rectangle with private width and height, a constructor that throws `invalid_argument` if either is not positive, const member functions `area()` and `perimeter()`, and `operator==` that compares areas. Show the results of `Rectangle(3, 4)` for area and perimeter, and what happens for `Rectangle(0, 5)`.\n\n**B2. (10 marks)** (a) Predict the output [4]:\n```cpp\nstruct X { X() { cout << \"X+ \"; } ~X() { cout << \"X- \"; } };\nstruct Y : X { Y() { cout << \"Y+ \"; } ~Y() { cout << \"Y- \"; } };\nint main() { Y y1; { X x; Y y2; } cout << \"| \"; }\n```\n(b) Explain the difference between a shallow and a deep copy, and why a class owning a raw pointer needs a user-defined copy constructor [3]. (c) State the Rule of Three [3].\n\n**B3. (10 marks)** Write an abstract class Animal with a pure virtual function `speak()` and a virtual destructor, classes Dog (\"Woof\") and Cat (\"Meow\"), store a Dog, a Cat and another Dog in a `vector<unique_ptr<Animal>>` and print all sounds in order. Give the output and explain how the right function is chosen at run time.\n\n**B4. (10 marks)** (a) Write a function template `T sumAll(const vector<T>& v)` and give the result for `{1, 2, 3, 4}` and `{1.5, 2.5}` [3]. (b) Predict the contents and size of v after: `vector<int> v = {4, 1, 3, 1, 4, 2}; sort(v.begin(), v.end()); v.erase(unique(v.begin(), v.end()), v.end());` [4] (c) What does `count_if(v.begin(), v.end(), [](int x){ return x % 2 == 0; })` return for v = {1, 2, 3, 4}? [3]\n\n---\n\n## Answer Key and Solutions\n\n### Section A\n1 **B** · 2 **C** · 3 **A** · 4 **B** · 5 **B** · 6 **B** · 7 **B** · 8 **A** · 9 **B** · 10 **C** · 11 **C** · 12 **B** · 13 **B** · 14 **C** · 15 **B** · 16 **B** · 17 **B** · 18 **A** · 19 **B** · 20 **B**\n\nBrief reasons: 4. Stack unwinding of scope. 8. Pass-by-value copies. 9. const member functions promise not to modify. 12. unique_ptr. 14. Amortised constant. 15. Balanced tree, sorted. 17. Otherwise deleting through Base* is undefined behaviour. 18. The stream is the left operand.\n\n### Section B\n\n**B1.**\n```cpp\nclass Rectangle {\npublic:\n    Rectangle(double w, double h) : w_(w), h_(h) {\n        if (w <= 0 || h <= 0) throw invalid_argument(\"dimensions must be positive\");\n    }\n    double area() const { return w_ * h_; }\n    double perimeter() const { return 2 * (w_ + h_); }\n    bool operator==(const Rectangle& o) const { return area() == o.area(); }\nprivate:\n    double w_, h_;\n};\n```\nRectangle(3, 4): **area 12, perimeter 14**. Rectangle(0, 5) **throws invalid_argument** from the constructor, so no invalid object ever exists (the invariant w, h > 0 is always true). (3 marks class design, 3 constructor check, 2 const methods, 2 results.)\n\n**B2.**\n(a) y1: X+ Y+ (base first). In the block: x: X+; y2: X+ Y+. At the end of the block the objects are destroyed in reverse order: y2 (Y- X-), then x (X-). Then \"| \". At the end of main: y1 (Y- X-). Output: **X+ Y+ X+ X+ Y+ Y- X- X- | Y- X- **.\n(b) A **shallow copy** copies member values, including pointer values, so both objects point to the same memory (aliasing); a **deep copy** allocates new memory and copies the contents. With the default shallow copy, modifying one object changes the other, and when both are destroyed the memory is deleted twice (undefined behaviour). A user-defined copy constructor performs the deep copy.\n(c) **Rule of Three:** if a class needs a user-defined destructor, copy constructor or copy assignment operator, it almost certainly needs all three (and, in modern C++, the move operations too: the Rule of Five).\n\n**B3.**\n```cpp\nclass Animal {\npublic:\n    virtual void speak() const = 0;\n    virtual ~Animal() = default;\n};\nclass Dog : public Animal { public: void speak() const override { cout << \"Woof \"; } };\nclass Cat : public Animal { public: void speak() const override { cout << \"Meow \"; } };\n\nint main() {\n    vector<unique_ptr<Animal>> zoo;\n    zoo.push_back(make_unique<Dog>());\n    zoo.push_back(make_unique<Cat>());\n    zoo.push_back(make_unique<Dog>());\n    for (const auto& a : zoo) a->speak();\n}\n```\nOutput: **Woof Meow Woof **. Each object contains a hidden pointer to its class's vtable; a call through an Animal pointer to a virtual function follows that pointer, so the function of the object's actual (dynamic) type runs. The virtual destructor makes the unique_ptr delete the correct derived object.\n\n**B4.**\n(a)\n```cpp\ntemplate <typename T>\nT sumAll(const vector<T>& v) {\n    T total{};                               // value-initialised: 0\n    for (const T& x : v) total += x;\n    return total;\n}\n```\nsumAll({1, 2, 3, 4}) = **10** (T = int); sumAll({1.5, 2.5}) = **4** (T = double).\n(b) After sort: 1 1 2 3 4 4. `unique` moves the first of each run of equal elements to the front and returns the new end; erase removes the tail: v = **{1, 2, 3, 4}**, size **4**.\n(c) The even elements are 2 and 4: **2**.\n"},mcq:[{"q":"Hiding the data of a class behind a public interface is called","options":["inheritance","encapsulation","overloading","templating"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.025,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Private members of a class can be accessed","options":["by any function","only by derived classes","by the class's own member functions (and friends)","only in main"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.075,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A constructor is called","options":["when an object is created","when an object is destroyed","when a function returns","never automatically"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.125,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Local objects are destroyed in","options":["the same order as construction","the reverse order of construction","random order","alphabetical order"],"answer":1,"explain":"Stack unwinding of scope.","f":0.175,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The keyword `virtual` on a member function enables","options":["faster calls","dynamic dispatch (run-time polymorphism)","private access","static linking"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.225,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"An abstract class is one that","options":["has no data members","has at least one pure virtual function","cannot have a constructor","has only static members"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.275,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"`class Dog : public Animal` expresses","options":["a has-a relationship","an is-a relationship","a uses-a relationship","no relationship"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.325,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The copy constructor is invoked when","options":["an object is passed by value to a function","an object is passed by reference","a pointer is passed","a static is created"],"answer":0,"explain":"Pass-by-value copies.","f":0.375,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A `const` member function","options":["cannot be called on a non-const object","promises not to modify the object's members","is always inline","must be static"],"answer":1,"explain":"const member functions promise not to modify.","f":0.425,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Compared with a pointer, a reference","options":["can be null","can be re-seated to another object","must be initialised and cannot be re-seated","takes more memory"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.475,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Memory allocated with `new[]` must be released with","options":["delete","free","delete[]","release"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.525,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Which smart pointer expresses sole ownership?","options":["shared_ptr","unique_ptr","weak_ptr","auto_ptr only"],"answer":1,"explain":"unique_ptr cannot be copied, so exactly one owner exists and the object is freed when that owner goes out of scope.","f":0.575,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Templates provide","options":["run-time polymorphism","generic programming resolved at compile time","garbage collection","multiple inheritance"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.625,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The amortised complexity of vector::push_back is","options":["O(n)","O(log n)","O(1)","O(n^2)"],"answer":2,"explain":"Most push_back calls cost O(1). The occasional resize copies everything but happens rarely, so the average is still O(1).","f":0.675,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"std::map stores its keys","options":["in insertion order","in sorted order","in random order","in reverse order"],"answer":1,"explain":"Balanced tree, sorted.","f":0.725,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The recommended way to catch an exception is","options":["by value","by const reference","by raw pointer","with catch(...) only"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.775,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A virtual destructor is required in a base class when","options":["the class has no data","objects are deleted through base-class pointers","the class is final","the class is a template"],"answer":1,"explain":"Otherwise deleting through Base* is undefined behaviour.","f":0.825,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The stream insertion operator `operator<<` for a class is normally","options":["a non-member (often friend) function","a static member","a constructor","a virtual function"],"answer":0,"explain":"The stream is the left operand.","f":0.875,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A static data member is","options":["copied into each object","shared by all objects of the class","always constant","only usable in main"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.925,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Assigning a derived-class object to a base-class object (by value) causes","options":["a compile error always","slicing: the derived part is lost","a run-time exception","nothing"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.975,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"In a C++ `class`, members are by default","options":["public","private","protected","static"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.025,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"In a C++ `struct`, members are by default","options":["public","private","protected","const"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.075,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Data members are initialised in the order","options":["of the member initialiser list","of their declaration in the class","alphabetical","undefined"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.125,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The keyword `explicit` on a one-argument constructor","options":["makes it faster","prevents implicit conversions","makes it private","makes it virtual"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.175,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Which member functions can be called on a `const` object?","options":["any function","only const member functions","only static functions","only constructors"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.225,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The purpose of `override` is to","options":["make a function virtual","have the compiler check that a base-class virtual function is really overridden","prevent inheritance","hide the base function"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.275,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A pure virtual function is declared with","options":["`= 0`","`= delete`","`final`","`static`"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.325,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The vtable in a polymorphic class is","options":["one per object","one per class","one per program","one per member function"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.375,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The diamond problem in multiple inheritance is solved with","options":["friend classes","virtual inheritance","templates","private inheritance"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.425,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The Rule of Three concerns","options":["destructor, copy constructor, copy assignment","three constructors","three base classes","three template parameters"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.475,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"`std::move(x)` does what?","options":["moves x immediately","casts x to an rvalue reference, allowing a move","deletes x","copies x"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.525,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"`shared_ptr::use_count()` returns","options":["the size of the object","the number of shared_ptr owners","the number of weak_ptrs only","the address"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.575,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A template specialisation provides","options":["a special implementation for a particular type","a virtual function","a new base class","a namespace"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.625,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"vector::reserve(n)","options":["changes the size to n","allocates capacity for n elements without changing the size","clears the vector","sorts n elements"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.675,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The average cost of a lookup in std::unordered_map is","options":["O(1)","O(log n)","O(n)","O(n log n)"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.725,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Which algorithm sorts a range?","options":["std::sort","std::find","std::accumulate","std::count"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.775,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Destructors should be","options":["allowed to throw","noexcept (never throw)","always virtual in every class","private"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.825,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A main drawback of the Singleton pattern is","options":["it uses too much memory","it introduces global state that hinders testing","it cannot be thread-safe","it needs templates"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.875,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The Observer pattern is used to","options":["create objects without naming classes","notify dependent objects when a subject changes","wrap an interface","copy objects"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.925,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The Open/Closed Principle says software entities should be","options":["open for modification, closed for use","open for extension, closed for modification","always final","always abstract"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.975,"src":"Final exam"}]});
